### Task 2: Fix the cap (only if Task 1 confirmed suspect 1)

**Files:** Modify `lib/generator/candidates.ts:84-102`

- [ ] **Step 1: The failing tests already exist** — Task 1's first two measurements are red.

- [ ] **Step 2: Implement breadth-first generation**

The cap must sample *across* the closet, not take a prefix of a top-major product. Rotate the
categories so each pass takes one combination per top before any top gets a second:

```ts
  const combos: CandidateItem[][] = [];
  const maxLen = Math.max(tops.length, bottoms.length, shoes.length);

  // Breadth-first over the tops. A nested triple loop generates every combo of
  // the FIRST top before reaching the second, so slicing to CAP kept only that
  // top — one line that made most of a large closet invisible to ranking and
  // made all three of the day's looks share a garment.
  outer: for (let d = 0; d < maxLen; d++) {
    for (let t = 0; t < tops.length; t++) {
      const b = bottoms[(t + d) % bottoms.length];
      const s = shoes[(t + d * 2) % shoes.length];
      if (!b || !s) continue;
      const base = needsOuterwear && outerwear.length
        ? [tops[t], b, s, outerwear[(t + d) % outerwear.length]]
        : [tops[t], b, s];
      combos.push(base);
      if (a.maxAccessories > 0 && accessories.length) {
        combos.push([...base, accessories[(t + d) % accessories.length]]);
      }
      if (combos.length >= CAP) break outer;
    }
  }
  return combos;
```

This also resolves suspect 2 — the modular indices mean coats and accessories rotate instead of being
pinned to index 0.

The in-season-first ordering from the seasons plan still holds: `tops`, `bottoms` and `shoes` arrive
sorted, so low `d` values pair the best-ranked pieces with each other.

